From: rcllf local patch
Subject: [PATCH] RTI and federate: stop a federate whose upstream left without resigning

When a federate leaves the federation with an error, its downstream federates
are never told. Under decentralized coordination the closed peer socket only
marks the departed peer's inputs absent until FOREVER (mark_inputs_known_absent),
so the downstream federate runs to its timeout with no input, logs nothing and
exits 0 (one federate exited 3 after 4 ms, the other ran its 30 s and
exited 0). Under centralized coordination the RTI only released the held TAGs
for a MSG_TYPE_FAILED, not for a federate whose connection simply closed.

How a departure with an error looks at the RTI: a clean end always sends
MSG_TYPE_RESIGN after the federation's stop. An exit() after an error sends
MSG_TYPE_FAILED (lf_terminate_execution with _lf_normal_termination false).
An _Exit() (the rcllf binding's fatal path, exit 3) or a crash sends nothing:
the RTI's read of that federate's connection fails.

RTI (rti_remote.c): on MSG_TYPE_FAILED, and on a connection that closes without
MSG_TYPE_RESIGN, the RTI sends the new MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED (200,
followed by the 16-bit ID of the federate that left and a one-byte direct
flag) to every other started federate (state GRANTED): direct = 1 when the
departed federate's neighbor structure lists it as an immediate downstream,
direct = 0 otherwise. The list alone is not enough. A decentralized federation
sends no neighbor structure (the generated lf_send_neighbor_structure_to_RTI
sends 0 upstreams and 0 downstreams), and the neighbor structure leaves
physical connections out, so under centralized coordination a federate fed
only through a physical connection (a P2P socket under any coordination) is
not listed. An earlier version of this patch told only the listed federates
when the list was not empty; such a federate then stopped only through a
notified sibling's stop request, logged nothing and exited 0
(a mixed physical-connection run with one federate killed). A closed connection is now handled like
MSG_TYPE_FAILED: the RTI marks its own exit as an error, sets the federate's
next event to FOREVER and releases the TAGs it held back.

Federate (federate.c): each federate records which federates opened an
inbound peer connection to it (at accept time; the record outlives the
connection, which a crashed peer closes before the RTI's notice arrives;
accessed atomically, since the accept thread writes it and the RTI listener
thread reads it). A notice cannot overtake that record: a sender's
lf_connect_to_federate returns only after the receiver recorded it and sent
the ACK, the sender sends its start timestamp after that, and the RTI starts,
and so notifies, no federate before every timestamp has arrived. On
MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED with direct = 1, or with direct = 0 from a
recorded inbound peer, it logs an error naming the federate, calls the weak
hook rcllf_upstream_failed_hook(fed_id) when the program defines it (the rcllf
binding does, and exits 3 at the end), and calls lf_request_stop(), so the
federation stops at a coordinated tag instead of running on. Any other notice
is logged as information and ignored. The federate records which federate's
departure stopped it, and lf_reactor_c_main (reactor_threaded.c) then returns
1 instead of 0 with an error line, so a plain LF federate without the hook
does not exit 0 either (it used to). Its termination is still normal: it
resigns. The rcllf binding's atexit shutdown ends the process with 3 after
main returned.

A federate stopped by this notice ends normally and resigns, so its own
downstream federates get no notice of their own; the stop request it sends
stops the whole federation at one tag instead.

The patch carries its own marker in both files: federated
programs compile federate.c, the RTI compiles rti_remote.c.

Upstream reactor-c has no such notice (checked against the pinned 2f63540d).

Rcllf-Marker-For: 0009-rti-federate-upstream-failed-notice
Rcllf-Marker-Scope: federated
---
--- a/core/federated/RTI/rti_remote.c
+++ b/core/federated/RTI/rti_remote.c
@@ -894,6 +894,54 @@
 }
 
 /**
+ * rcllf: tell the started federates that `failed` left without
+ * resigning. Without this a downstream federate runs on with the inputs from
+ * `failed` absent and records nothing. Every other started federate gets the
+ * notice: direct = 1 when the neighbor structure lists it as an immediate
+ * downstream of `failed`, direct = 0 otherwise, and a direct = 0 receiver
+ * decides by its own inbound peer connections. Not only the listed ones: the
+ * neighbor structure leaves physical connections out (and decentralized
+ * federations send none), so a federate fed by `failed` only through a
+ * physical connection is not in the list.
+ * The caller holds rti_mutex. A failed write is only logged.
+ * @param failed The federate that left.
+ */
+static void notify_downstream_of_failure(federate_info_t* failed) {
+  {
+    // rcllf patch marker: the benchmark harness reads it back from the running program.
+    static const char rcllf_marker[] __attribute__((section("rcllf_reactor_c_patches"), used, unused)) =
+        "rcllf-reactor-c-patch:0009-rti-federate-upstream-failed-notice";
+  }
+  {
+    // rcllf patch marker: the benchmark harness reads it back from the running program.
+    static const char rcllf_marker[] __attribute__((section("rcllf_reactor_c_patches"), used, unused)) =
+        "rcllf-reactor-c-patch:0009-rti-federate-upstream-failed-notice";
+  }
+  unsigned char buffer[MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED_LENGTH];
+  buffer[0] = MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED;
+  encode_uint16((uint16_t)failed->enclave.id, &buffer[1]);
+  for (int i = 0; i < rti_remote->base.number_of_scheduling_nodes; i++) {
+    federate_info_t* d = GET_FED_INFO(i);
+    if (d == failed || d->enclave.state != GRANTED || d->net == NULL) {
+      continue;
+    }
+    bool direct = false;
+    for (int j = 0; j < failed->enclave.num_immediate_downstreams; j++) {
+      if (failed->enclave.immediate_downstreams[j] == i) {
+        direct = true;
+        break;
+      }
+    }
+    buffer[3] = direct ? 1 : 0;
+    lf_print_error("RTI: Federate %d left without resigning. Telling %s federate %d.", failed->enclave.id,
+                   direct ? "downstream" : "started", d->enclave.id);
+    if (write_to_net(d->net, MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED_LENGTH, buffer)) {
+      lf_print_warning("RTI: Failed to tell federate %d that federate %d left.", d->enclave.id, failed->enclave.id);
+    }
+  }
+}
+
+/**
  * Handle MSG_TYPE_FAILED sent by a federate. This message is sent by a federate
  * that is exiting in failure.  In this case, the RTI will
  * also terminate abnormally, returning a non-zero exit code when it exits.
@@ -914,6 +962,7 @@
   _lf_federate_reports_error = true;
   lf_print_error("RTI: Federate %d reports an error and has exited.", my_fed->enclave.id);
 
+  notify_downstream_of_failure(my_fed);
   my_fed->enclave.state = NOT_CONNECTED;
 
   // Indicate that there will no further events from this federate.
@@ -988,7 +1037,19 @@
     if (read_failed) {
       // network abstraction is closed
       lf_print_info("RTI: Connection to federate %d is closed. Exiting the thread.", my_fed->enclave.id);
+      // rcllf: the connection closed without MSG_TYPE_RESIGN (an
+      // _Exit or a crash): handle it as MSG_TYPE_FAILED. Mark the error, tell the
+      // started federates downstream of it, and release the TAGs it held back.
+      LF_MUTEX_LOCK(&rti_mutex);
+      _lf_federate_reports_error = true;
+      lf_print_error("RTI: Federate %d closed its connection without resigning.", my_fed->enclave.id);
+      notify_downstream_of_failure(my_fed);
       my_fed->enclave.state = NOT_CONNECTED;
+      my_fed->enclave.next_event = FOREVER_TAG;
+      bool* visited = (bool*)calloc(rti_remote->base.number_of_scheduling_nodes, sizeof(bool));
+      notify_downstream_advance_grant_if_safe(&(my_fed->enclave), visited);
+      free(visited);
+      LF_MUTEX_UNLOCK(&rti_mutex);
       // Nothing more to do. Close the network abstraction and exit.
       // Prevent multiple threads from closing the same network abstraction at the same time.
       shutdown_net(my_fed->net, false);
--- a/core/federated/federate.c
+++ b/core/federated/federate.c
@@ -1538,6 +1538,90 @@
 static void handle_rti_failed_message(void) { exit(1); }
 
 /**
+ * rcllf: the program may define this hook (the rcllf ROS 2 binding
+ * does) to record that an upstream federate left and to fail its own exit.
+ */
+extern void rcllf_upstream_failed_hook(int fed_id) __attribute__((weak));
+
+/**
+ * rcllf: the federates that opened an inbound peer connection to
+ * this one, recorded at accept time. Unlike net_for_inbound_p2p_connections it
+ * is not cleared when the connection closes, because a crashed peer's
+ * connection usually closes before the RTI's notice arrives. Written by the
+ * P2P accept thread and read by the RTI listener thread, so both sides use
+ * atomic accesses. A notice cannot overtake the record: the sender's
+ * lf_connect_to_federate returns only after this federate recorded it and sent
+ * the ACK, the sender sends its start timestamp after that, and the RTI starts
+ * (and so notifies) no federate before every timestamp has arrived.
+ */
+static bool rcllf_inbound_peer[NUMBER_OF_FEDERATES];
+
+/**
+ * rcllf: the federate whose departure stopped this
+ * one, -1 while none did. Written by the RTI listener thread, read by the main
+ * thread when lf_reactor_c_main returns.
+ */
+static int rcllf_stopped_by_federate = -1;
+
+/**
+ * rcllf: the exit code lf_reactor_c_main returns for
+ * a run that ended normally: 1 when an upstream-failed notice stopped this
+ * federate, 0 otherwise. Without it a plain LF federate stopped by the notice
+ * exited 0, so its upstream's failure read as a clean run. The federate still
+ * resigns (the termination is normal), so its own downstream federates get no
+ * notice; a program that defines rcllf_upstream_failed_hook (the rcllf
+ * binding) may end the process with its own code later (exit 3).
+ * @return 1 or 0.
+ */
+int rcllf_upstream_failed_exit_code(void) {
+  int fed_id = __atomic_load_n(&rcllf_stopped_by_federate, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
+  if (fed_id < 0) {
+    return 0;
+  }
+  lf_print_error("Exiting with code 1: federate %d, which sends to this federate, left the federation without "
+                 "resigning.",
+                 fed_id);
+  return 1;
+}
+
+/**
+ * rcllf: handle MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED from the RTI. A
+ * federate left without resigning. When it sends to this one (the RTI says so,
+ * or it opened an inbound peer connection here), its inputs here stay absent
+ * from now on: log it, call the program's hook and request a stop instead of
+ * running on silently. Otherwise log it and run on.
+ */
+static void handle_upstream_failed_message(void) {
+  {
+    // rcllf patch marker: the benchmark harness reads it back from the running program.
+    static const char rcllf_marker[] __attribute__((section("rcllf_reactor_c_patches"), used, unused)) =
+        "rcllf-reactor-c-patch:0009-rti-federate-upstream-failed-notice";
+  }
+  unsigned char buffer[MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED_LENGTH - 1];
+  read_from_net_fail_on_error(_fed.net_to_RTI, MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED_LENGTH - 1, buffer,
+                              "Failed to read an upstream-failed message from the RTI.");
+  int fed_id = (int)extract_uint16(buffer);
+  bool upstream = buffer[2] != 0 || (fed_id >= 0 && fed_id < NUMBER_OF_FEDERATES &&
+                                      __atomic_load_n(&rcllf_inbound_peer[fed_id], __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE));
+  if (!upstream) {
+    lf_print("Federate %d left the federation without resigning. It does not send to this federate; running on "
+             "(rcllf).",
+             fed_id);
+    return;
+  }
+  lf_print_error("Federate %d, which sends to this federate, left the federation without resigning (an error exit "
+                 "or a crash). Stopping this federate.",
+                 fed_id);
+  int none = -1;
+  __atomic_compare_exchange_n(&rcllf_stopped_by_federate, &none, fed_id, false, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL,
+                              __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE); // rcllf
+  if (rcllf_upstream_failed_hook != NULL) {
+    rcllf_upstream_failed_hook(fed_id);
+  }
+  lf_request_stop();
+}
+
+/**
  * Thread that listens for network abstraction inputs from the RTI.
  * When messages arrive, this calls the appropriate handler.
  * @param args Ignored
@@ -1603,6 +1687,9 @@
     case MSG_TYPE_FAILED:
       handle_rti_failed_message();
       break;
+    case MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED:
+      handle_upstream_failed_message();
+      break;
     case MSG_TYPE_CLOCK_SYNC_T1:
     case MSG_TYPE_CLOCK_SYNC_T4:
       lf_print_error("Federate %d received unexpected clock sync message from RTI.", _lf_my_fed_id);
@@ -2117,6 +2204,9 @@
     // Otherwise, there can be race condition where, during termination,
     // two threads attempt to simultaneously close the network abstraction.
     _fed.net_for_inbound_p2p_connections[remote_fed_id] = net;
+    if (remote_fed_id < NUMBER_OF_FEDERATES) {
+      __atomic_store_n(&rcllf_inbound_peer[remote_fed_id], true, __ATOMIC_RELEASE); // rcllf
+    }
 
     // Send an MSG_TYPE_ACK message.
     unsigned char response = MSG_TYPE_ACK;
--- a/core/threaded/reactor_threaded.c
+++ b/core/threaded/reactor_threaded.c
@@ -1128,6 +1128,13 @@
   if (ret == 0) {
     LF_PRINT_LOG("---- All environment worker threads exited successfully.");
   }
+#ifdef FEDERATED
+  // rcllf: a federate stopped because an upstream
+  // federate left without resigning returns 1, not 0 (federate.c).
+  if (ret == 0) {
+    ret = rcllf_upstream_failed_exit_code();
+  }
+#endif
   _lf_normal_termination = true;
   return ret;
 }
--- a/include/core/federated/federate.h
+++ b/include/core/federated/federate.h
@@ -597,4 +597,11 @@
 instant_t lf_wait_until_time(tag_t tag);
 #endif // FEDERATED_DECENTRALIZED
 
+/**
+ * @brief rcllf: the exit code of a federate whose run
+ * ended normally: 1 when the RTI's upstream-failed notice stopped it, else 0.
+ * @ingroup Federated
+ */
+int rcllf_upstream_failed_exit_code(void);
+
 #endif // FEDERATE_H
--- a/network/api/net_common.h
+++ b/network/api/net_common.h
@@ -736,6 +736,23 @@
  */
 #define MSG_TYPE_DOWNSTREAM_NEXT_EVENT_TAG 26
 
+/**
+ * @brief rcllf: byte identifying that a federate left the
+ * federation without resigning.
+ * @ingroup Network
+ *
+ * Sent by the RTI when a federate sends MSG_TYPE_FAILED or its connection to
+ * the RTI closes without a MSG_TYPE_RESIGN. The next two bytes are the ID of
+ * the federate that left; the byte after them is 1 when the receiver is one of
+ * its immediate downstream federates by the neighbor structure, 0 when it is
+ * not listed there. Every started federate gets the notice: the
+ * neighbor structure leaves physical connections out, and a decentralized
+ * federation sends none. A receiver of a 0 notice decides by its own inbound
+ * peer connections.
+ */
+#define MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED 200
+#define MSG_TYPE_RCLLF_UPSTREAM_FAILED_LENGTH 4
+
 /////////////////////////////////////////////
 //// Rejection codes
 
